WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.237
Quantum risk assessment should be specific, not one-size-fits-all.

00:00:03.237 --> 00:00:06.766
Useful factors include data sensitivity, data lifetime, system lifetime,

00:00:06.766 --> 00:00:09.511
cryptographic dependency, exposure, adversary interest, replacement difficulty,

00:00:09.511 --> 00:00:11.472
vendor readiness, and business impact.

00:00:11.472 --> 00:00:17.746
A public website with short-lived data may not have the same urgency as a sensitive archive,

00:00:17.746 --> 00:00:22.844
a firmware signing system, or an industrial device with a twenty-year deployment cycle.

00:00:22.844 --> 00:00:28.726
Data sensitivity asks what harm would occur if the information were exposed in the future.

00:00:28.726 --> 00:00:32.255
Data lifetime asks how long confidentiality must be preserved.

00:00:32.255 --> 00:00:36.569
System lifetime asks how long the system will remain in use.

00:00:36.569 --> 00:00:40.882
Cryptographic dependency asks whether the system relies on quantum-vulnerable public-key algorithms.

00:00:40.882 --> 00:00:46.764
Exposure asks whether traffic or artifacts can be collected or tampered with by relevant adversaries.

00:00:46.764 --> 00:00:49.509
Replacement difficulty is often the deciding factor.

00:00:49.509 --> 00:00:55.391
If a system can be updated quickly through a well-tested library and normal deployment pipeline,

00:00:55.391 --> 00:00:59.705
it may be lower priority than a system with hard-coded cryptography,

00:00:59.705 --> 00:01:01.665
vendor-controlled firmware, or regulatory constraints.

00:01:01.665 --> 00:01:06.763
Vendor readiness matters because many organizations cannot change product cryptography on their own.

00:01:06.763 --> 00:01:10.292
Business impact matters because migration failures can disrupt operations.

00:01:10.292 --> 00:01:13.037
A good assessment produces a prioritized roadmap.

00:01:13.037 --> 00:01:16.567
It should identify high-risk data, high-dependency systems, hard-to-change platforms,

00:01:16.567 --> 00:01:18.527
trust anchors, and vendor-controlled components.

00:01:18.527 --> 00:01:23.625
It should also identify lower-risk areas where routine lifecycle updates may be enough.

00:01:23.625 --> 00:01:27.546
The point is to avoid both complacency and uncontrolled urgency.

00:01:27.546 --> 00:01:31.860
Quantum readiness should be disciplined, evidence-based, and tied to real systems.
